CS 214 Project I: My little malloc()

Authors
-------
Sriya Ghankot  (sg2104)
Isabella D'Amato  (iad24)

Building and running
--------------------
    make            build everything (memgrind, memtest, correct, errors)
    ./memgrind      stress test, reports average workload time
    make test       run every correctness/error test and memgrind
    make clean      remove object files and executables

Design notes
------------
* The heap is a static 4096-byte (MEMLENGTH) array wrapped in a union with
  a double to guarantee 8-byte alignment. MEMLENGTH may be overridden at
  compile time (e.g. -DMEMLENGTH=8192).
* Every chunk starts with an 8-byte header: an int holding the total chunk
  size (header + payload) and an int allocated flag. Chunks are contiguous,
  so the next chunk is at (header address + size): an implicit linked list
  starting at the beginning of the array. No explicit pointers are stored.
* Request sizes are rounded up to a multiple of 8 with (n + 7) & ~7, so
  every chunk and payload is a multiple of 8 and every returned pointer is
  8-byte aligned. Minimum chunk size is 16 bytes.
* malloc() uses first fit. If the chosen chunk has at least 16 bytes left
  over, it is split and the remainder stays free; otherwise the whole chunk
  is handed out (the extra bytes are padding).
* malloc(0), and any request larger than MEMLENGTH - 8, fail with the
  standard "Unable to allocate" message and return NULL.
* free() walks the chunk list from the start, tracking the previous chunk.
  It only accepts a pointer that exactly equals the payload address of an
  allocated chunk. Anything else -- a pointer outside the heap, a pointer
  into the middle of a chunk, or a pointer to an already-free chunk -- is
  reported as "free: Inappropriate pointer (file:line)" and the process
  exits with status 2. The walk compares pointers only for equality, so
  pointers outside the heap are handled without undefined behavior.
* free(NULL) does nothing, matching the standard free(). This lets client
  code safely free the result of a failed malloc().
* free() coalesces the freed chunk with a free successor and a free
  predecessor, so two adjacent free chunks never exist.
* Initialization is lazy: the first call to malloc() or free() sets up a
  single free chunk spanning the heap and registers the leak checker with
  atexit(). A single static int records whether this has happened.
* The leak checker counts allocated chunks at exit and prints
  "mymalloc: N bytes leaked in M objects." to stderr. N counts payload bytes
  (including alignment padding) but not headers. It never calls exit().

Test plan
---------
For each requirement we state how a violation would show up, and which
test checks it.

1. malloc() reserves unallocated memory (objects don't overlap).
   Detection: fill each object with a distinct byte, then check that
   every object still holds its own byte.
   Tests: memtest (64 objects filling the heap exactly), correct
   "allocated objects do not overlap".

2. Returned pointers are 8-byte aligned.
   Detection: pointer value % 8 != 0 for any request size.
   Test: correct "payloads are 8-byte aligned" (sizes 1, 4, 7, ... 28).

3. free() deallocates memory.
   Detection: if free() leaked chunks, repeatedly allocating the entire
   heap would fail after the first time.
   Test: correct "free() deallocates memory for reuse" (100 rounds of
   allocating and freeing a 4088-byte object).

4. Adjacent free chunks are coalesced.
   Detection: after filling the heap with small chunks and freeing them
   all (in a non-sequential order), a single heap-sized allocation fails
   if any neighbors were not merged.
   Tests: correct "adjacent free chunks are coalesced"; memgrind task 4.

5. malloc() returns NULL and reports an error when it can't fit a request.
   Detection: a request that can't fit returns non-NULL, or no message is
   printed.
   Test: correct "malloc() returns NULL when out of memory" (a 4096-byte
   request, and a second 2048-byte request when only 2032 bytes are left).

6. The allocator never touches allocated payloads.
   Detection: an object's contents change while other objects are
   allocated and freed around it.
   Test: correct "object data unchanged by other allocations".

7. free() detects bad pointers and exits with status 2.
   Detection: the error message is missing or the exit status isn't 2.
   Test: errors 1 (address of a local variable), errors 2 (pointer into
   the middle of a chunk), errors 3 (double free). "make test" checks
   that each exits with status 2.

8. Leaks are reported at exit.
   Detection: the expected leak message is missing.
   Test: errors 4 leaks malloc(20) and malloc(100) (24 + 104 bytes after
   rounding); "make test" checks for "128 bytes leaked in 2 objects".

9. free(NULL) is accepted and does nothing, as with the standard free().
   Detection: the program reports an error or exits with status 2.
   Test: errors 5; "make test" checks that it exits with status 0.

10. Client code works unchanged with or without mymalloc.h.
   Detection: memtest fails to build or run with the real malloc().
   Test: "make test" compiles memtest with -DREALMALLOC and runs it.

11. The library holds up under a large number of operations.
   Test: memgrind (below). Any corruption would show up as a failed
   malloc, a spurious "Inappropriate pointer" error, or a leak report.

Test programs
-------------
memtest      Provided sample client. Fills the heap with 64 objects of 56
             bytes, checks the contents, frees them. No arguments.

correct      Runs the correctness tests above, printing PASS/FAIL for each.
             Exits 0 if all pass. No arguments. The two
             "Unable to allocate" messages it prints are expected.

errors N     Triggers one error case:
               1  free() an address not from malloc()
               2  free() a pointer not at the start of a chunk
               3  free() the same pointer twice
               4  leak two objects (leak report at exit, exit status 0)
               5  free(NULL), which must be a harmless no-op (exit status 0)
             Cases 1-3 should print "free: Inappropriate pointer" and exit
             with status 2. (Case 2 also prints a leak report, since the
             chunk is still allocated when the process exits.)

memgrind     Records the start time with gettimeofday(), runs the workload of
             five tasks 50 times, records the end time, and prints the
             average time per workload (elapsed time / 50). No arguments.
               Task 1: allocate 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 512, 1024 bytes, free
                       them in reverse order.
               Task 2: allocate 120 1-byte objects, free them in order.
               Task 3: randomly choose between allocating a 1-byte object
                       and freeing a random live one, until 120 allocations
                       have been made, then free the rest.
               Task 4: allocate 120 8-byte objects, free the odd indices,
                       then the even ones (each even free must coalesce
                       with both neighbors), then allocate and free a
                       2048-byte object, which only fits if coalescing
                       worked.
               Task 5: build a 60-node linked list, delete every other node,
                       insert new nodes of varying sizes into the gaps, then
                       free the whole list from the front.
